"In Driven, the function generator under the picture sets the <strong>Drive frequency f</strong> with its <strong>Coarse</strong> ({#} Hz a click) and <strong>Fine</strong> ({#} Hz a click) knobs or its slider, from {#} f₁ to {#} f₁ in {#} Hz steps; dragging the red marker on the response plot sweeps it. <strong>Quality factor Q of mode {#}</strong> runs from {#} to {#} ({#} in the setup). Readouts: <strong>Wave speed v = √(T/μ)</strong>, <strong>Fundamental f₁ = v/2L</strong>, the steady amplitude with the damping time {#}/γ, and a table of the harmonics.",
"<strong>Record reading</strong> works in the String view with Harmonic or Driven. It stores the mode, the harmonic number (left blank in Driven for you to fill in from the antinodes you count), the frequency, the steady amplitude in mm (Driven), L, T, μ and Q. <strong>Export to worksheet</strong> fills the Data table in order: Scan (the row and mode), n, f, Amplitude, L, T, μ.",
"Beats view: two pure tones. <strong>Frequency f₁</strong>, {#} to {#} Hz in {#} Hz steps; <strong>Difference f₂ − f₁</strong>, −{#} to {#} Hz in {#} Hz steps; <strong>Amplitude A₂ / A₁</strong>. The two upper strips zoom in on about five cycles of each tone and of their sum at the playhead; the long strip shows the band the sum fills, its envelope (dashed) and a bracket labeled T_beat between two loud moments. Readouts: <strong>Frequency f₂</strong>, <strong>Pitch heard (f₁ + f₂)/{#}</strong>, <strong>Beat frequency |f₁ − f₂|</strong> and <strong>Beat period</strong>. With <strong>Sound</strong> on both tones play.",
"<strong>Mystery tone: f₂ hidden</strong> draws a new f₂ at random between {#} and {#} Hz and hides the difference slider, f₂, the pitch heard, the beat frequency and the beat period; the T_beat bracket goes away and the long strip becomes a fixed {#} s window with a time axis, so the beats in it are yours to count. Ticking it again draws a new f₂; unticking it shows f₂.",
"An ideal flexible string: small amplitude, fixed ends, no stiffness, so \\(v = \\sqrt{\\frac{T}{\\mu}},\\qquad f_n = \\frac{n}{2L}\\sqrt{\\frac{T}{\\mu}}.\\) Harmonic and pluck motion have no damping, and the pluck is a sum of {#} modes. Up and down displacement is exaggerated; in 3D the string is drawn three times thicker than a {#} mm guitar string and the hanging mass is \\(m = T/g\\) with \\(g = 9.81\\ \\text{m/s}^2\\).",
"The driven string is a point force \\(F_0\\cos(2\\pi f t)\\) with \\(F_0 = 0.25\\ \\text{N}\\) at \\(x = L/20\\), with the same damping rate \\(\\gamma = \\omega_1/Q\\) for every mode, solved exactly with {#} modes, transients included. Each peak has a full width at half power of \\(\\gamma/2\\pi\\) in Hz, and the amplitude needs a few damping times \\(2/\\gamma\\) ({#} ms at Q = {#} in the setup) to settle; the response plot is the steady state amplitude against f.",
